A||||6||86SE||2.8 Cam in a 3.4 = Performance Cam??||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||08-18-2001||01:46 AM||||I heard that the 2.8 Cam fits into the 3.4L engine and gives it an extra 10-15 HP.  Anyone else heard of this?  I cant find a 3.4 performance cam anywhere and it would be cool if this would work.<P>Thanks<BR>Chuck<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg">   <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>V-6 w/rod knock Yellow Fiero<BR> -- 3.4L Conversion *Back in session!* --||||reg||5
Z||000001||c m west||08-18-2001||07:59 AM||||we sell a performance cam, visit out website<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||00lE||08-18-2001||08:14 AM||||The blocks are identical except for the increased bore on the 3.4, so any cam designed to fit the 2.8 will also fit the 3.4 block.  Given this, it should be obvious that the "stock" cam from a Fiero MPFI 2.8 will not yield ANY horsepower increase if it were transplanted into a camaro/firebird 3.4. :-0  There are many relatively inexpensive off the shelf performance cams available for the 60 degree V6, and these cams will work just fine in either motor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||08-18-2001||09:05 AM||||I remember researching cams and their relation to engine displacement back when I was selecting a cam for my engine build. <P>I no longer have this information memorized, but do remember something about how an "econo" cam for one particular engine displacement, is classified as a "performance" cam for a different displacement, or vice/versa. I can type the info up later today if interested. I just need to get my books open again.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||08-18-2001||02:01 PM||||The Crane PowerMax H-260-2 camshaft is the best camshaft for the 3.4-liter pushrod motor in my opinion. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dave Gunsul||08-18-2001||03:41 PM||||The 3.4 comes stock with the Crane 260-2 cam.<BR>Chevy just renames it with their own part number. I would think that you could safetly<BR>use the H-272-2 cam and gain some extra power. You could also use 1.6 rockers on the original cam and basically get the same gain as switching to the bigger cam. Some people don't like the bigger rockers though so the cam change might be best for your application.<P><BR>Just for reference the stock 3.4 cams specs are: 204 int. 216 exh. @.50 with .427 int. .454 exh. gross lift.<BR>The H-272-2 specs are: 216 int. 228 exh. @.50<BR>with .454 int. .480 exh. gross lift.<BR>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 08-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT GT 87||08-18-2001||04:19 PM||||If you search GM Hi-perf , you will see the cam specs. I bought the 12364059 cam kit (includes lifters) for 108$ from a GM dealer. It has the same specs as the 3.4 but with a little better lobe seperation. Make sure you use the Cloyes true roller timing chain set- worth 2-4 H.P.||||reg||1
